Now F2b line 245: "C2 is a strictly larger extension class, so C2-inextendibility is strictly weaker" — this says E_C2 is larger than *this class* (C0), which is false: E_C2 ⊂ E_C0. The correct statement is exactly what the taxonomy says about C^{1,1}/H^2_loc relative to *C2*: those are larger than C2. So the F2b row conflated the reference class: relative to C0, C2 is the *smaller* extension class (fewer extensions), and C2-inextendibility is weaker — the outcome is right, the reason is inverted.

Also F2b line 188 and 190 say "a C2 result is weaker" and "C0-inextendibility is the stronger statement" — consistent with my reading. So line 245's reason contradicts lines 188, 190, 238, 242.

Also interesting: F2a line 243 says "the converse containment is false; C2-inextendibility is weaker than H2_loc-inextendibility" — consistent.

Now, is the defect "major"? The lead says: "L-FORM-01 (major, hash-bound to 55d0a1ea): ... states 'C2 is a strictly larger extension class', contradicting the same file's line 238 containment E_C0 contains E_H2loc contains E_{C^1,1} contains E_C2, where C2 is the SMALLER extension class; sibling F2a states the same row correctly".

So my task: independently verify + characterize propagation + controls + falsifier. Let me also check F1 (af_wcc_vacuum) for analogous "extension class" ordering claims (WCC is a different family, probably no such chain). And check the F0 taxonomy's class-contract supplement for the same row.

Also check the artifact/formulation/schemas mirror copies (they're byte-identical mirrors, so same defect) and any other copies of the C0 schema (e.g., in worker snapshots — those are snapshots, not live). And check whether the same defect exists in earlier revisions still archived: not needed.

Let me search for all occurrences of "strictly larger" and the containment string across all live canonical paths and mirrors.

