Regex for containment chain: find the line with `extension_class_containment:`. Extract quoted string. Then tokenize occurrences of `E_...` and the relations `contains`/`subset of` between consecutive tokens. Simplest: split the string on the keywords while capturing order. I'll implement:

```python
def norm_class(tok):
    t = tok.strip()
    t = t.replace('E_','')
    t = t.replace('{','').replace('}','').replace('\\','')
    t = t.replace('^','')  # C^1,1 -> C1,1
    return t
```
Then find all `E_[{A-Za-z0-9^,}\\]*` matches with positions and the relation words between consecutive matches. If relation contains "contains" → previous is larger than next (earlier in O). If "subset of" → previous is smaller (later in O).

For C0 string: "E_C0 contains E_H2loc contains E_{C^1,1} contains E_C2" → tokens [C0,H2loc,C1,1,C2] with 3 "contains" → O=[C0,H2loc,C1,1,C2]. Expected set equality and chain direction. For F2a: "E_C2 subset of E_{C^1,1} subset of E_H2loc subset of E_C0" → O=[C2,C1,1,H2loc,C0] reversed → largest-first = [C0,H2loc,C1,1,C2]. Good. Supplement line 145: "extension sets are nested E_C2 subset of E_{C^1,1} subset of E_H2loc subset of E_C0" → same. Note the token regex must not catch "E_C2 subset of E_{C^1,1}" weirdly; handle `{`/`}` in token by matching `E_[A-Za-z0-9^{},]+`.

Also the containment lines might contain other E_ tokens outside the chain (e.g., "E_{C^1,1}"). Fine.

For B: scan all lines of each file for regex `([A-Za-z0-9^,{}_\\]+) is a strictly (larger|smaller) extension class`. Normalize class token: it may be "C2" or "C^{1,1}". Normalize to canonical keys via a mapping: {'C0':'C0','H2loc':'H2loc','H^2_loc':'H2loc','C^1,1':'C1,1','C^{1,1}':'C1,1','C1,1':'C1,1','C2':'C2'}. The sentence "those are strictly larger classes" (plural, no single token) won't match the singular regex — that's fine; add a separate check for taxonomy line 140 manually via a plural regex `(those|they) are strictly larger classes` with context "does NOT forbid C^{1,1} or H^2_loc extensions" → assert presence and correctness relative to *C2* (the subject of meaning_C2). I'll implement a targeted taxonomy check: parse meaning_C2's "strictly larger classes" and verify the two named classes (C^{1,1}, H^2_loc) are larger than C2 per O.

For C: regex `([A-Za-z0-9^,{}_\\]+)-inextendibility is (strictly )?(stronger|weaker) than ([A-Za-z0-9^,{}_\\ ]+?)-inextendibility` and also "X is stronger than the Y class's conclusion" variants. Keep it focused: C0 line 242 `reason: "transitivity of the two rows above; C0-inextendibility is stronger than the C2 class's conclusion"`; C0 line 190 `a C2-inextendibility proof does NOT subsume this class's conclusion. The implication runs C0 => C2 only: C0-inextendibility is the stronger statement, so a C2 result is weaker`; F2a line 243; supplement line 145. I'll parse:
  - `([\w^{},]+)-inextendibility is (?:the )?(strictly )?(stronger|weaker) than (?:the )?([\w^{},]+)` → relation.
  - `([\w^{},]+)-inextendibility is the (stronger|weaker) statement` → relative to the file's own class.
  - `implication runs ([A-Za-z0-9]+) => ([A-Za-z0-9]+)` → entailment direction: X => Y means S_X entails S_Y means S_X stronger.
  - `([A-Za-z0-9]+) containment runs ([A-Za-z0-9]+) => ([A-Za-z0-9]+) only` (C0 line 271).
  Rule: "S_X stronger than S_Y" ⟺ idx(X) < idx(Y); "weaker" ⟺ idx(X) > idx(Y); "X => Y" ⟺ idx(X) < idx(Y).

